    <h1>Jixin vs Xiaomi: Which Extra-Hot Chilli to Choose</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Jixin and Xiaomi are the two workhorse extra-hot varieties at 50,000–70,000 SHU. The heat band is identical — the difference is pod shape, origin and how each behaves in a dish or blend.
    </p>

    <div class="takeaways">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> Jixin (chicken-heart) is a small heart-shaped pod from Guizhou and Hunan, the standard for extreme-heat products and Sichuan-style dishes, dosed as an accent by weight. Xiaomi (millet) is a small, slender pod from Yunnan and Guizhou whose compact size concentrates heat — it stays visible whole in dishes or as segments.
    </div>
    <AtAGlance facts={glanceFacts} />

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">The core difference</h2>
    <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin-top:8px;">
      <thead>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #d8c9c0;text-align:left;">
          <th style="padding:8px;">Dimension</th>
          <th style="padding:8px;">Jixin (chicken-heart)</th>
          <th style="padding:8px;">Xiaomi (millet)</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Shape</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Small heart-shaped</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Small, slender</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Region</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Guizhou, Hunan</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Yunnan, Guizhou</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Heat (SHU)</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">50,000–70,000</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">50,000–70,000</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Best for</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Extreme-heat products, chilli oil, Sichuan-style dishes, hot-pot bases</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">High-heat whole chillies, segments, chilli oil</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>In dishes</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Dosed as an accent by weight</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Compact pod stays visible</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Why the choice matters</h2>
    <p>
      Both are extra-hot at 50,000–70,000 SHU — see the <a href="/heat-levels/extra-hot/">extra hot</a> page. <a href="/products/jixin-chilli/">Jixin</a> is the reference for extreme heat: small heart-shaped pods from Guizhou and Hunan, standard for extreme-heat products, chilli oil and Sichuan-style dishes. <a href="/products/xiaomi-chilli/">Xiaomi</a> is a small, slender pod from Yunnan and Guizhou whose compact size concentrates heat, so it delivers strong heat from a small, visible pod — used whole, as segments, or in chilli oil.
    </p>
    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Dose math: why extra-hot is blended, not used neat</h2>
    <p>
      To land a 10,000-SHU blend, Jixin (50,000–70,000 SHU) is roughly 14–20% of the recipe by weight — about 1 part Jixin to 4–6 parts base. Dose by weight, because heat is non-linear and a small over-dose moves the blend far more than a medium chilli would.
    </p>
    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">How to specify</h2>
    <p>
      Name the variety and a precise SHU window, and confirm the form — segments and powder are most common at this heat level. Extra-hot varieties are usually blended, not used neat.
    </p>
    <p class="data-note">
      Heat and colour values are representative. Confirm your exact SHU window, ASTA, form and mesh
      when you inquire.
    </p>

